The phrase "quantum mechanics" refers to an area of physics that emerged around 1925 with the development of wave mechanics by Erwin Schrödinger and further expansion by many others, including significant contributions from physicists such as Max Born, Werner Heisenberg, Wolfgang Pauli, Paul Dirac, Richard Feynman, among others. The term itself was coined in German ("Quantenmechanik") during the early 1920s at the University of Göttingen by a group that included these physicists and P. Jordan.

